  "findings_resolved": [
    {
      "id": "R1-PL-prism-product",
      "resolution": "Finite base-skeleton PL trivialization: vertex fibers, boundary rectangle maps, triangle prism maps by matching cone fans over the same base coordinates. Deck-equivariant choices give a descended PL middle section and twisted cores."
    },
    {
      "id": "R1-empty-ball-remainder",
      "resolution": "Interior sphere-ball excludes proper components by original boundary; punctured closed remainders by loops essential in the original positive-genus component. Boundary-ball remainders retain original fundamental group after restoring a disk along one interval, hence an essential loop contradicts injection."
    },
    {
      "id": "R1-hierarchy-family-transfer",
      "resolution": "Use V=whole S1 subfamily contained in product P. Any family boundary-compressing disk misses all outside components as well, so it would compress original S1. Apply simultaneous-family product classification, not unsupported componentwise boundary incompressibility."
    },
    {
      "id": "R1-joint-PL-cutoff",
      "resolution": "Use L(s,r)=min(s,lambda(r)), finite PL on a triangulated parameter square. Compose with the supplied jointly PL face isotopy; endpoint and inverse maps are PL."
    },
    {
      "id": "R1-relative-annulus-link-clarification",
      "resolution": "Same reviewer requested this clarification before successor freeze, within the same bundle: finite proper-arc link-disk straightening gives compatible boundary collars; an inward PL collar embedding moves accidental inner-end boundary contacts; a sufficiently low fixed collar-height circle and its vertical extension normalize outer end. Hemisphere/half-link arc straightening then proves actual relative product/quadrant charts before applying the collar supplier. No new neatness assumption."
    }
  ],
